Construct complex arithmetic and identify its algebraic limits (@math-complex-arithmetic: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "Relate-complex-products-powers-and-roots-to-rotations.lingua#r_007A555DF9E363BDCFDBD9976D") { r_006DD1FF362C1C4973327211C7
Define a complex number as an ordered pair of real numbers. Addition is (a,b)+(c,d)=(a+c,b+d); multiplication is (a,b)(c,d)=(ac−bd,ad+bc). The pair (a,0) represents a real number, and i=(0,1) satisfies i²=(−1,0). Thus write (a,b)=a+bi. The real part is a and the imaginary part is the real coefficient b, not the term bi. This construction defines multiplication explicitly rather than relying on an unexplained square root symbol.

Pair addition is associative and commutative. The multiplication formula is symmetric in its factors, so multiplication is commutative. Expanding either grouping of (a+bi)(c+di)(e+fi) gives real part ace−bde−adf−bcf and imaginary part acf−bdf+ade+bce, proving associativity. Distribution over addition follows by expanding coordinates. The multiplicative identity is (1,0), and (0,0) is the additive identity. These computations explain the algebraic structure rather than just checking a few examples.

Define conjugate z̄=a−bi and |z|=√(a²+b²). Then zz̄=a²+b²=|z|². For z≠0, its inverse is z̄/|z|² because the positive denominator is nonzero. Consequently division by c+di multiplies by c−di and divides both resulting coordinates by c²+d². Expanding the product norm proves |zw|²=|z|²|w|², hence |zw|=|z||w| since norms are nonnegative. A product of nonzero complex numbers cannot vanish.

**Original calculation:** (2−i)(3/5+4i/5)=2+i. Both sides have squared norm 5 because the second factor has norm one. Recover 2−i by division. Compare this with the coordinate rotation already derived; the same formula now has an algebraic interpretation.

Some real-number habits fail. There is no total order extending the usual real order and compatible with the field operations: squares would have to be nonnegative, but i²=−1 contradicts that requirement. Nor can the positive-real square-root product rule be applied indiscriminately: choosing √(−1)=i gives √(−1)√(−1)=−1 while √((-1)(-1))=1. An equation z²=−1 has the two solutions ±i; a selected square-root value is a convention, not the full solution set.

**Read and practice:** [Algebra and Trigonometry 2e — Abramson and contributors](../../inquiry/quantitative-literacy/sources/Algebra-and-Trigonometry.lingua#r_7FKH7S2VNZT97STHJ10KB186YX), section 2.4. Prove the conjugate of a product is the product of the conjugates, compute a quotient by both direct multiplication and the inverse formula, and identify every excluded denominator. Explain why a complex extension solves some equations without proving the much stronger fundamental theorem of algebra.

